<h2>The dispute</h2>

<p>DMRC awarded a consortium led by L&T a January 2008 contract for the design, supply, installation and commissioning of 25kV rigid overhead contact system switching stations for Delhi MRTS Phase-II. L&T procured high-voltage panels imported through Germany and low-voltage panels from Kolkata, paid Delhi VAT of Rs 4,45,73,743 on the sectioning and sub-sectioning post items, and sought reimbursement under Clause 24(c)(ii). DMRC's position was that the sales were in the course of import and inter-State trade — outside DVAT under Section 7 of the DVAT Act read with Sections 3 and 5 of the Central Sales Tax Act — so nothing was reimbursable. The tribunal agreed with DMRC and returned a NIL award in March 2014. L&T challenged it under Section 34.</p>

<h2>The court's findings</h2>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Jurisdiction objection rejected</h3><p>Whether DVAT applied to these supplies, as between the parties, fell within the arbitrable category; the sovereign-function argument did not oust the tribunal.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Governing authority applied</h3><p>The tribunal followed the Division Bench's ABB framework; the conditions showing a direct link between the inter-State procurement, the import and the contract were satisfied.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Nexus not severed</h3><p>Integrating the purchased panels into sectioning posts did not break the integral connection between the movement of goods and the contract.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Section 34 discipline</h3><p>No re-appreciation of evidence; a plausible view based on the record stands, and mere incorrect application of law is not sufficient for interference.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The Section 34 standard, restated</h2>

<p>The judgment collects the familiar limits of a setting-aside court in commercial arbitrations: the award must reflect due application of mind; a plausible view on the material is immune; patent illegality must go to the root of the matter; and the court does not sit in appeal over the tribunal's appreciation of evidence or its construction of the contract, so long as the tribunal has not rewritten the bargain. Each of those phrases appears in the court's reasoning, and together they explain why a decade-old NIL award on a crore-level claim survived.</p>

<h2>Drafting and strategy lessons for works contracts</h2>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Issue</th><th>Lesson</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Tax reimbursement clauses</td><td>Tie reimbursement to taxes "properly leviable and paid", and prescribe a consultation mechanism before a party pays a disputed levy</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Procurement structure</td><td>Where goods move inter-State or in the course of import against the contract, the taxing consequence follows the movement — document the linkage either way</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Forum</td><td>Raise jurisdictional objections before the tribunal under Section 16; holding them back weakens a later Section 34 attack</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Refund strategy</td><td>If a tax was paid that was not exigible, pursue the statutory refund route promptly — limitation under tax statutes is unforgiving</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Although the dispute concerned the pre-GST DVAT/CST regime, the architecture of the reasoning carries forward: GST-era contracts raise the same questions about which supplies attract which levy, who bears a tax paid under protest, and whether the arbitral tribunal or the tax authority decides. The answer this judgment gives — the tribunal decides the inter-party contractual consequence, the tax code governs refunds — remains the working rule.</p>

<div class="note"><p>This article summarises a Delhi High Court judgment under Section 34 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 for general information. Tax positions under repealed statutes and transitional regimes require specific advice; nothing here is legal or tax advice.</p></div>
